            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news on NASA’s Mars Perseverance rover is:\n\n- **Perseverance has found evidence of ancient asteroid impacts on Mars.** NASA/JPL reported on **July 15, 2026** that the rover studied a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick** stack of layered rock on the rim of Jezero Crater, called the **“Broom Point member,”** which appears to have been built by repeated asteroid impacts more than **3.9 billion years ago**【6:1†source】.  \n- This terrain is significant because it may preserve a very old record of the early solar system. NASA notes that Mars’ lack of plate tectonics means ancient crustal records can remain intact in ways they usually do not on Earth【6:1†source】.  \n- NASA also highlighted new Perseverance imagery and mapping from the Broom Point area, including a sweeping view of Jezero Crater’s rim and the rover’s trip to the site【6:2†source】.  \n- Another recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a **full marathon — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — on Mars**, reaching that distance on its **1,890th Martian day**, or sol. It did so in about five years and four months, much faster than Opportunity reached the same distance【6:0†source】.  \n\nIn short: Perseverance is now exploring some of the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover, while also having just passed a major driving milestone on the Red Planet.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-reads-record-of-ancient-mars-impacts/",
